Note: This needs to be added in the line after `New_Ret` is assigned.
We assume that, if LastIndexOf is non-negative, the element is actually in the list. This means that if we find something in the list for the first time, it counts as a positive return value. If an element is removed from the list, however, the return value of LastIndexOf for that element will be negative. Therefore, it is good practice to check that the element actually is in the list before accepting a return value of 0 or higher for the same element. If a different behavior is expected, appropriate assumptions would need to be made.--------------------As of now, no assertions seem to be included directly in the given code. However, the comments starting with "// @@@" suggest that we will need to include an assertion that New_Ret is non-negative to verify the scenario that x is in the old array list. 

Here is how the code might look using Debug.Assert in C#:
```cs
using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            // Assert that the return value is non-negative when x is in the old array list
            // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListIndexOfX >= 0, "x is not in the old array list");

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);
        }
    }
}
```

This code asserts that when x is in arrList, the value of New_Ret (which equals the index of the last occurrence of x in arrList) is non-negative. If it isn't, it throws an AssertionException with the message "x is not in the old array list".--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret != -1, "Non-negative return value is required, but it was found that x is not in the array list.");
```--------------------Here are the output assert statements corresponding to the "@@@" comments in the given code:

```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount >= Old_arrListIndexOfX); // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list
```

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX != -1 || !Old_arrListContainsX); // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list
Debug.Assert(New_arrListIndexOfX != -1); // @@@ If the return value is non-negative, x is in the old array list
```

Note: The second assertion checks for both conditions because `IndexOf` method can return either the position of the element or -1 if the element is not found. The third assertion checks if the element is no longer in the list after it's been removed.--------------------